            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Gerry Higgins"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Love this as it but also because it's easy to customize to taste. I used beef broth instead of water. Also if you want a more north east style bbq swap half the splenda for sugar-free maple syrup. Hotter add more tobacco or a dash of cayenne pepper. Smoked paprika never goes amiss in bbq sauce.",
            "datePublished": "2015-05-29T13:23:33.560Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Matt"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It's a good sauce, but it definitely can go with only 12oz of tomato paste. As is, it's way too thick, especially once refrigerated. It also might be a little too \"tomatoey\" for the people used to commercial BBQ sauces, but that's just a few tweaks away IMO. Also, I used Erythritol with monk fruit from Whole Foods for a sweetener. No weird fake sweeteners flavors from that.",
            "datePublished": "2024-08-18T11:12:28.589Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This sauce was definitely better after sitting in the fridge for a day. I used honey instead of Splenda, which I realize makes it not sugar free, but a family member feels sick if she eats refined sugar or sugar substitute. I added some garlic to the recipe and 1/2 T more of the liquid smoke. I am making it again today for baked pork chops",
            "datePublished": "2023-05-06T22:11:30.044Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jacqueline Koppenhaver"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a great recipe for anyone what wants to cut back on sugar in there BBQ sauce. My cans of tomato paste had 3 gram of sugar in each can and I used Jalapeno mustard with no sugar and our Worcester sauce had less then 1 gram. I also used Ham bouillon for flavor but the rest I left just as the recipe calls for. Thanks for posting. Will make again.",
            "datePublished": "2015-08-02T07:49:21.253Z"
